  "content": {
    "id": "p42-q33-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "33",
    "prompt": "The equation $$x^3+px^2+qx+r=0$$ has roots $\\alpha-2$, $\\alpha$ and $\\alpha+2$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p42-q33-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{p}{3}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Using the sum of roots, $3\\alpha=-p$, so $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{p}{3}$.",
          "worked": "Using the sum of roots,\n$$\\begin{align*}(\\alpha-2)+\\alpha+(\\alpha+2)&=-p\\\\3\\alpha&=-p\\\\\\alpha&=-\\frac{p}{3}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q33-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that $p^2=3(q+4)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$p^2=3(q+4)$.\n\nThe sum of roots in pairs gives $q=3\\alpha^2-4$; substitute $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{p}{3}$.",
          "worked": "Using the sum of roots in pairs,\n$$\\begin{align*}q&=(\\alpha-2)\\alpha+(\\alpha-2)(\\alpha+2)+\\alpha(\\alpha+2)\\\\q&=\\alpha^2-2\\alpha+\\alpha^2-4+\\alpha^2+2\\alpha\\\\q&=3\\alpha^2-4\\\\q&=3\\left(-\\frac{p}{3}\\right)^2-4\\\\q&=\\frac{p^2}{3}-4.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $p^2=3(q+4)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      }
    ]
  },
